<div class="p2c">

<p><b>This is not a replacement for the <a href="https://github.com/desertwitch/par2cron" target="_blank">full documentation</a>.</b><br>
It is a condensed guide meant to get you started and as a cheatsheet.</p>

<h2>Getting started:</h2>
<p>Place a <code>_par2cron</code> marker file in any folder you want protected.<br>
par2cron will create PAR2 parity data, periodically verify integrity, and repair corruption.</p>

<h2>Protecting a folder:</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Navigate to a folder containing files you want to protect</li>
  <li>Create an empty file named <code>_par2cron</code></li>
  <li>Done - a PAR2 set is created on the next scheduled run</li>
</ol>
<p>After creation of the PAR2 set, the marker file is deleted automatically.</p>

<h2>Marker file name modifiers (optional):</h2>
<p>Append <code>par2</code> arguments to the marker file name to override creation defaults for that folder:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Marker file name</th><th>Resulting arguments (assuming defaults: <code>-r15 -n1</code>)</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>_par2cron</code></td><td>Uses defaults: <code>-r15 -n1</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>_par2cron_r30</code></td><td>30% redundancy: <code>-r30 -n1</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>_par2cron_q</code></td><td>Quiet mode added: <code>-r15 -n1 -q</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>_par2cron_r30_q</code></td><td>Both changes: <code>-r30 -n1 -q</code></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Marker file content modifiers (optional):</h2>
<p>For full control, you can put YAML directives inside the marker file:</p>
<pre>name: "Ubuntu"        # PAR2 set name (folder mode only)
args: ["-r30", "-n1"] # Replaces default par2 arguments
glob: "*.iso"         # Protect only the matching files
mode: "folder"        # "folder" or "file" (see below)
verify: true          # Verify PAR2 set as part of creation
hidden: true          # Create PAR2 set as hidden (dotfiles)</pre>
<p>All directives are optional - only specify what you need to override.</p>

<h2>Creation modes:</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>folder</strong> (default): One PAR2 set for entire directory</li>
  <li><strong>file</strong>: Separate PAR2 set per file (useful for large files)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Ignoring folders:</h2>
<ul>
  <li><code>.par2cron-ignore</code> - skip this folder</li>
  <li><code>.par2cron-ignore-all</code> - skip this folder and all subfolders</li>
</ul>
<p>A present ignore-file excludes the folder from all par2cron commands.</p>

<h2>Re-creating a PAR2 set:</h2>
<p>If you added/changed/removed protected files:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Delete existing PAR2-related files (see below)</li>
  <li>Create an empty marker file named <code>_par2cron</code></li>
  <li>Done - a new PAR2 set is created on the next scheduled run</li>
</ol>
<p><b>Beware: par2cron is aimed primarily at non-changing content (media/backups).</b></p>

<h2>Explanation of the generated files:</h2>
<pre>Pictures/
|-- beach.jpg
|-- sunset.jpg
|-- Pictures.par2           # PAR2 index file
|-- Pictures.vol00+01.par2  # PAR2 recovery data
|-- Pictures.par2.json      # par2cron manifest
+-- Pictures.par2.lock      # par2cron lockfile</pre>
<p>If the amount of visible files bothers you, you can switch on <code>hidden</code> creation.</p>

</div>
